    "message": {
     "content": "### 1. Assessment: Is This a Good Offer?\n\n**Yes, it is a solid baseline offer, but as an initial package, it is essentially a lateral move from Meta.** \n\n* **The Good:** An equity grant of $1.08M at L6 is calibrated around the 50th\u201360th percentile for Google PMs. The target bonus is higher than Meta\u2019s standard IC6 target (20% vs. 15%).\n* **The Gaps:** \n  1. **Base salary cut:** Your base drops from $240k to $235k.\n  2. **No sign-on bonus:** External L6 hires from Tier-1 tech firms almost always receive a sign-on bonus to offset unvested equity forfeit and transition risk.\n  3. **Refresher dependency:** The recruiter\u2019s $250k/year refresher illustration represents a strong baseline scenario, but because refreshers depend on performance ratings and company macro-budgets, you shouldn't rely on uncontracted equity to justify a move.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Side-by-Side Comparison\n\n| Component | Current Meta (IC6) | Offered Google (L6) | Notes |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $235,000 | -$5,000 cut (Band max is $271k) |\n| **Target Bonus** | 15% (~$36,000) | 20% ($47,000) | +$11,000 target cash |\n| **Equity (Year 1)** | ~$200,000\u2013$250,000 | $270,000* | *Assuming flat 25% vest (see note below) |\n| **Sign-on Bonus** | N/A | $0 | Standard gap to close |\n| **Year 1 Target TC** | **~$476,000\u2013$526,000** | **~$552,000** | Only a **~5\u201310% bump** over current TC |\n\n> **Important Detail on Google Equity Vesting:**  \n> Verify with the recruiter whether this grant follows a standard flat 4-year vest (25%/yr) or Google's front-loaded schedule (e.g., **38% / 32% / 20% / 10%** or **33% / 33% / 22% / 12%**). If front-loaded at 38%, your Year 1 equity is **$410,400** (bringing Year 1 TC to ~$692k), but Year 4 drops significantly before refreshers kick in.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Recommendation: What Should You Do?\n\n**Negotiate. Do not accept the initial offer, and do not walk away yet.**\n\n* **You hold significant leverage:**\n  * Google approached you as a passive candidate.\n  * You are happy and well-compensated at Meta.\n  * Meta IC6 and Google L6 are direct equivalents; they know what Meta pays at this tier.\n* Google does not rescind offers for polite, well-reasoned counters from strong candidates.\n\n---\n\n### 4. What Exactly to Ask For\n\nWhen countering, target an annualized package that clearly beats your current trajectory:\n\n| Lever | Current Offer | Target Counter | Rationale / Anchor |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $235,000 | **$250,000 \u2013 $255,000** | You should not take a base pay cut; $255k is safely within their posted band ($183k\u2013$271k). |\n| **Sign-on Bonus** | $0 | **$75,000 \u2013 $100,000** | Offsets leaving unvested equity/refresher momentum behind at Meta. |\n| **Equity (GSUs)** | $1,080,000 | **$1,200,000** (~$300k/yr) | Pushes your package toward the top quartile for L6 PMs. |\n\n---\n\n### 5. Negotiation Script / Email Draft\n\nYou can deliver this over the phone or follow up via email:\n\n> *\"Hi [Recruiter Name],*\n>\n> *Thank you again for putting this offer together. I\u2019ve enjoyed meeting the team, and I\u2019m very excited about the scope of the [Team/Product] PM role.*\n>\n> *Because I wasn't actively looking and have strong momentum and established equity vesting at Meta, compensation is an important factor in making a transition.*\n>\n> *Comparing the numbers, the current base salary ($235k) represents a slight step down from my current base ($240k), and without a sign-on bonus, the overall Year 1 differential is very narrow relative to what I am walking away from.*\n>\n> *To make this an easy decision and get to an immediate signature, I would be looking for:*\n> 1. *A **base salary of $255,000** (to reflect my seniority and ensure positive cash-flow growth).*\n> 2. *An **initial equity grant of $1,200,000** over 4 years.*\n> 3. *A **sign-on bonus of $75,000** to offset forfeited unvested stock and transition risk.*\n>\n> *If we can bridge the gap to these numbers, I\u2019d be thrilled to sign and focus on my start date.*\n>\n> *Could you let me know what flexibility the compensation committee has here?\"*\n\n---\n\n### Summary Checklist Before Signing\n1. **Clarify the GSU vesting schedule** (flat 25%/yr vs. front-loaded 38/32/20/10).\n2. **Prioritize Sign-on and Base:** Recruiters often find it easiest to grant a sign-on bonus first, followed by equity expansion, and base salary last.\n3. **Hold out for at least a $50k+ sign-on bonus** and a match/exceed on your $240k base.",
